    "answer": {
      "short": "$6\\text{ cm}$; double the half-diagonal $\\sqrt{5^2-4^2}=3\\text{ cm}$.",
      "worked": "The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles. Each resulting right-angled triangle has hypotenuse $5\\text{ cm}$ and one shorter side of length $4\\text{ cm}$. Let $l$ be half the unknown diagonal and $d$ its full length.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{8}{2}&=4\\text{ cm}\\\\l&=\\sqrt{5^2-4^2}\\\\l&=3\\text{ cm}\\\\d&=2\\times3\\\\d&=6\\text{ cm}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
